1
00:00:00,060 --> 00:00:03,080
Before 1846, the best surgeon in the world

2
00:00:03,160 --> 00:00:06,900
was simply the fastest one. Why? Because

3
00:00:06,940 --> 00:00:08,980
the patient was awake for every second of

4
00:00:09,060 --> 00:00:11,900
it, and in Europe and America, nobody knew

5
00:00:11,940 --> 00:00:13,080
how to stop the pain.

6
00:00:14,280 --> 00:00:16,379
In London, one surgeon was famous for his

7
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:18,900
speed. His students timed him with their

8
00:00:18,940 --> 00:00:20,900
pocket watches, and he could take off a

9
00:00:20,960 --> 00:00:24,160
leg in just a few minutes. Then in one

10
00:00:24,260 --> 00:00:27,560
week in the autumn of 1846, all of that

11
00:00:27,620 --> 00:00:28,200
changed.

12
00:00:29,320 --> 00:00:31,900
In Boston, a dentist held a glass globe to

13
00:00:31,960 --> 00:00:34,460
a young man's face, and the young man felt

14
00:00:34,580 --> 00:00:35,360
nothing at all.

15
00:00:36,320 --> 00:00:38,540
So how do you put a person safely to sleep

16
00:00:38,880 --> 00:00:40,060
and bring them back again?

17
00:00:40,960 --> 00:00:43,540
Who worked it out first? And why did the

18
00:00:43,580 --> 00:00:45,560
men who found it spend the rest of their

19
00:00:45,620 --> 00:00:47,160
lives arguing about it?

20
00:00:48,280 --> 00:00:50,460
By the end of this film, you'll know how a

21
00:00:50,520 --> 00:00:53,600
party trick, a country doctor, and a room

22
00:00:53,680 --> 00:00:56,160
full of doubters turned surgery from a

23
00:00:56,200 --> 00:00:58,460
race into something you could sleep

24
00:00:58,520 --> 00:00:58,820
through.

25
00:01:00,140 --> 00:01:02,860
Picture yourself in 1840 about to have an

26
00:01:02,900 --> 00:01:06,320
operation. There's no injection, no mask,

27
00:01:06,660 --> 00:01:09,600
no sleep, just a strong drink perhaps and

28
00:01:09,640 --> 00:01:11,340
a few people to hold you still.

29
00:01:12,500 --> 00:01:13,800
What would you want most from your

30
00:01:13,860 --> 00:01:14,360
surgeon?

31
00:01:15,240 --> 00:01:17,580
Not a gentle touch. Speed.

32
00:01:18,660 --> 00:01:20,420
Surgeons had wished for something better

33
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:23,180
for centuries, and the strange thing is

34
00:01:23,520 --> 00:01:25,360
the answer had already been written down

35
00:01:25,400 --> 00:01:28,900
nearly fifty years earlier. In 1800 in

36
00:01:28,940 --> 00:01:31,500
Bristol, a young chemist called Humphry

37
00:01:31,580 --> 00:01:34,300
Davy was breathing a gas called nitrous

38
00:01:34,380 --> 00:01:34,960
oxide.

39
00:01:35,900 --> 00:01:38,140
It made him giddy and made him laugh.

40
00:01:39,040 --> 00:01:41,300
People soon called it laughing gas.

41
00:01:42,160 --> 00:01:43,740
Davy noticed something else.

42
00:01:44,700 --> 00:01:47,200
When he had a sore tooth, the gas seemed

43
00:01:47,220 --> 00:01:48,260
to take the pain away,

44
00:01:49,180 --> 00:01:51,180
so he wrote that it might be useful during

45
00:01:51,300 --> 00:01:52,640
surgical operations.

46
00:01:53,620 --> 00:01:56,620
Nobody followed it up. Instead, laughing

47
00:01:56,720 --> 00:02:00,040
gas and another vapor called ether became

48
00:02:00,080 --> 00:02:00,980
party tricks.

49
00:02:02,120 --> 00:02:04,320
Traveling showmen let audiences breathe

50
00:02:04,360 --> 00:02:07,540
them on stage, and young people held ether

51
00:02:07,640 --> 00:02:10,259
frolics where guests staggered around

52
00:02:10,380 --> 00:02:13,700
laughing. And at those parties, something

53
00:02:13,780 --> 00:02:16,440
odd kept happening. People would fall

54
00:02:16,520 --> 00:02:19,760
over, bang their knees and elbows, and not

55
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:21,700
feel a thing until the next morning.

56
00:02:22,560 --> 00:02:25,500
In a small town in Georgia, a young doctor

57
00:02:25,620 --> 00:02:28,920
called Crawford Long noticed. In March

58
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:32,580
1842, a patient called James Venable

59
00:02:32,940 --> 00:02:34,840
needed a lump removed from his neck.

60
00:02:35,900 --> 00:02:38,660
Long gave him ether on a towel and cut.

61
00:02:39,620 --> 00:02:42,420
Venable felt nothing. It worked.

62
00:02:43,320 --> 00:02:46,220
But Long didn't tell the wider world. He

63
00:02:46,240 --> 00:02:48,200
didn't publish what he'd done for another

64
00:02:48,300 --> 00:02:51,120
seven years. So if a country doctor had

65
00:02:51,180 --> 00:02:54,160
already done it quietly, who would be the

66
00:02:54,200 --> 00:02:55,620
one to show everyone?

67
00:02:56,520 --> 00:02:59,140
In Hartford, Connecticut, a dentist called

68
00:02:59,180 --> 00:03:01,880
Horace Wells saw a laughing gas show in

69
00:03:01,920 --> 00:03:05,820
December 1844. The next day, he breathed

70
00:03:05,880 --> 00:03:08,440
the gas himself and had a colleague pull

71
00:03:08,520 --> 00:03:12,060
out one of his own teeth. He felt nothing.

72
00:03:12,800 --> 00:03:14,220
Wells was thrilled.

73
00:03:15,080 --> 00:03:16,960
A few weeks later, he went to a famous

74
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:19,080
hospital in Boston to show the doctors

75
00:03:19,160 --> 00:03:22,720
there. It went wrong. The gas wasn't

76
00:03:22,760 --> 00:03:26,060
enough, and the patient said it hurt. The

77
00:03:26,140 --> 00:03:29,300
students in the room called it a humbug, a

78
00:03:29,500 --> 00:03:29,920
fake.

79
00:03:30,900 --> 00:03:32,880
But Wells' old business partner was in

80
00:03:32,940 --> 00:03:35,520
that room, a young dentist called William

81
00:03:35,620 --> 00:03:38,560
Morton, and he didn't give up on the idea.

82
00:03:39,520 --> 00:03:42,880
He tried a different vapor instead, ether.

83
00:03:43,500 --> 00:03:47,340
On the sixteenth of October, 1846, Morton

84
00:03:47,440 --> 00:03:49,480
walked into the same Boston hospital

85
00:03:49,940 --> 00:03:53,180
carrying a glass globe. A young man called

86
00:03:53,320 --> 00:03:55,760
Edward Abbott had a lump on his neck.

87
00:03:56,580 --> 00:03:59,280
Morton held the globe to his mouth, and

88
00:03:59,440 --> 00:04:02,400
Abbott breathed the ether in and slowly

89
00:04:02,440 --> 00:04:03,160
fell asleep.

90
00:04:04,080 --> 00:04:06,620
The surgeon, John Collins Warren, began.

91
00:04:07,320 --> 00:04:10,940
Abbott didn't scream. He didn't move. When

92
00:04:10,980 --> 00:04:13,340
it was over, Warren is said to have turned

93
00:04:13,400 --> 00:04:15,040
to the packed room and told them,

94
00:04:15,040 --> 00:04:18,220
"Gentlemen, this is no humbug."

95
00:04:19,300 --> 00:04:21,300
The room where it happened is still there.

96
00:04:21,720 --> 00:04:24,760
It's called the Ether Dome, and within

97
00:04:24,820 --> 00:04:27,300
weeks the news was crossing the Atlantic.

98
00:04:28,040 --> 00:04:31,780
A happy ending then? Case closed? Wrong.

99
00:04:32,700 --> 00:04:34,620
Morton didn't want to give his discovery

100
00:04:34,680 --> 00:04:37,800
away. He colored the ether and hid its

101
00:04:37,880 --> 00:04:41,480
smell so nobody could tell what it was. He

102
00:04:41,560 --> 00:04:44,840
called it Letheon after a river in the old

103
00:04:44,920 --> 00:04:48,140
Greek stories that made people forget, and

104
00:04:48,180 --> 00:04:49,640
he took out a patent on it.

105
00:04:50,580 --> 00:04:53,200
But doctors soon worked out it was ether.

106
00:04:53,920 --> 00:04:55,860
You can't keep a secret like that in a

107
00:04:55,900 --> 00:04:56,800
busy hospital.

108
00:04:58,100 --> 00:05:00,660
A month after the operation, a doctor and

109
00:05:00,780 --> 00:05:03,620
poet called Oliver Wendell Holmes wrote

110
00:05:03,720 --> 00:05:04,700
Morton a letter.

111
00:05:05,900 --> 00:05:08,180
He suggested a new word for this sleep

112
00:05:08,220 --> 00:05:09,000
without pain,

113
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:13,520
anesthesia, from the Greek for without

114
00:05:13,680 --> 00:05:14,160
feeling.

115
00:05:15,040 --> 00:05:18,340
Then the arguing started. Morton said he

116
00:05:18,400 --> 00:05:21,760
was first. His old teacher, a chemist

117
00:05:21,860 --> 00:05:24,400
called Charles Jackson, said the idea had

118
00:05:24,440 --> 00:05:27,820
been his. Horace Wells said he'd done it

119
00:05:27,880 --> 00:05:30,380
two years earlier. And from Georgia,

120
00:05:30,800 --> 00:05:33,440
Crawford Long said he'd done it before any

121
00:05:33,500 --> 00:05:33,880
of them.

122
00:05:34,780 --> 00:05:37,180
The fight dragged on for years in

123
00:05:37,260 --> 00:05:39,880
newspapers and in courtrooms, and none of

124
00:05:39,920 --> 00:05:42,440
the four men ended it rich or happy.

125
00:05:43,360 --> 00:05:45,200
So who really was first?

126
00:05:46,140 --> 00:05:48,720
In a way, Boston gave its answer.

127
00:05:49,680 --> 00:05:51,740
In the city's public garden, there's a

128
00:05:51,820 --> 00:05:54,960
tall stone monument to ether. It

129
00:05:55,020 --> 00:05:57,580
celebrates the discovery, and it doesn't

130
00:05:57,640 --> 00:05:59,220
name a single one of them.

131
00:06:00,340 --> 00:06:03,240
Meanwhile, across the ocean, the fastest

132
00:06:03,320 --> 00:06:05,540
surgeon in London was about to try it for

133
00:06:05,580 --> 00:06:10,420
himself. On the 21st of December 1846 in

134
00:06:10,460 --> 00:06:13,300
London, Robert Liston tried ether for

135
00:06:13,340 --> 00:06:13,960
himself.

136
00:06:15,080 --> 00:06:16,720
His patient slept through the whole

137
00:06:16,780 --> 00:06:17,520
operation.

138
00:06:18,560 --> 00:06:21,020
When he woke, he is said to have asked

139
00:06:21,560 --> 00:06:22,620
when it would begin.

140
00:06:23,640 --> 00:06:24,800
It was already over.

141
00:06:26,360 --> 00:06:29,160
Liston was delighted. He is said to have

142
00:06:29,220 --> 00:06:31,780
told the room, "This Yankee dodge,

143
00:06:31,920 --> 00:06:34,780
gentlemen, beats mesmerism hollow."

144
00:06:36,360 --> 00:06:39,140
The fastest knife in London no longer

145
00:06:39,220 --> 00:06:40,440
needed to be fast.

146
00:06:41,960 --> 00:06:44,500
The next year in Edinburgh, a doctor

147
00:06:44,600 --> 00:06:47,240
called James Young Simpson went looking

148
00:06:47,260 --> 00:06:48,640
for something even better.

149
00:06:49,940 --> 00:06:52,240
One evening, he and two friends sniffed a

150
00:06:52,300 --> 00:06:54,700
liquid called chloroform at his dinner

151
00:06:54,740 --> 00:06:58,160
table, and moments later, all three were

152
00:06:58,240 --> 00:06:59,560
out cold on the floor.

153
00:07:00,900 --> 00:07:03,540
When they woke, Simpson knew he'd found

154
00:07:03,620 --> 00:07:03,820
it.

155
00:07:05,060 --> 00:07:09,660
Not everyone trusted it. Then in 1853, a

156
00:07:09,760 --> 00:07:12,540
London doctor called John Snow gave

157
00:07:12,640 --> 00:07:15,380
chloroform to Queen Victoria for the birth

158
00:07:15,420 --> 00:07:17,520
of her son, Prince Leopold.

159
00:07:18,720 --> 00:07:20,220
If it was good enough for the Queen,

160
00:07:20,340 --> 00:07:22,560
people decided, it was good enough for

161
00:07:22,620 --> 00:07:22,940
them.

162
00:07:24,140 --> 00:07:27,040
But here's the frightening question: Is it

163
00:07:27,140 --> 00:07:29,980
safe to be put to sleep like that? Doesn't

164
00:07:30,020 --> 00:07:31,360
it sometimes go wrong?

165
00:07:32,720 --> 00:07:35,840
Today, for a healthy adult, deaths caused

166
00:07:35,880 --> 00:07:37,960
by an anesthetic are very rare,

167
00:07:38,880 --> 00:07:41,220
about one in every hundred thousand.

168
00:07:42,240 --> 00:07:45,420
Imagine a stadium full of 90,000 people

169
00:07:46,060 --> 00:07:48,100
all going to sleep for an operation.

170
00:07:49,020 --> 00:07:51,500
On average, fewer than one of them would

171
00:07:51,560 --> 00:07:53,300
die because of the anesthetic.

172
00:07:54,640 --> 00:07:56,920
That's because a specially trained doctor,

173
00:07:57,360 --> 00:08:00,220
an anesthetist, stays beside you the whole

174
00:08:00,340 --> 00:08:00,760
time.

175
00:08:01,620 --> 00:08:03,760
They watch every heartbeat and every

176
00:08:03,880 --> 00:08:06,020
breath, and they keep adjusting the

177
00:08:06,080 --> 00:08:09,040
medicine so you stay just asleep enough

178
00:08:09,460 --> 00:08:10,220
and no more.

179
00:08:11,560 --> 00:08:14,560
Why was this so groundbreaking? Because it

180
00:08:14,640 --> 00:08:16,499
changed what surgery could be.

181
00:08:17,600 --> 00:08:20,240
Once the patient slept, surgeons could

182
00:08:20,340 --> 00:08:24,060
slow down, take care, and try operations

183
00:08:24,120 --> 00:08:26,380
that nobody would ever have dared before.

184
00:08:27,720 --> 00:08:29,440
But there was still one problem.

185
00:08:30,340 --> 00:08:32,380
Many patients slept peacefully through

186
00:08:32,420 --> 00:08:35,380
their operations and then fell ill a few

187
00:08:35,420 --> 00:08:36,200
days later.

188
00:08:37,360 --> 00:08:38,840
The answer to that would need a

189
00:08:38,900 --> 00:08:42,540
microscope, a spray, and a surgeon called

190
00:08:42,620 --> 00:08:43,600
Joseph Lister.

191
00:08:44,660 --> 00:08:47,540
A gas, a bag, and a globe.

192
00:08:48,440 --> 00:08:51,340
Together, they turned surgery from a race

193
00:08:51,840 --> 00:08:52,680
into a sleep.
